From 8a65d0bd8082c8dc7ba330e4d59e848be8ae8add Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mingo <mingo@example.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:57:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(generate): report the lexer states and the transitions
 they drop

Every lexer state is built per *set* of tokens valid in the parse states that
start in it, so a transition that only some of the paths through the state can
take is dropped: where one token completes, another token's longer match is not
viable, and a separator transition (an extra) loses to a completed token at
equal precedence. That last rule is why `1 /* c */` lexes the `/` as a
division operator when a token can complete on `/`.

None of this is visible today: `--report-states-for-rule` shows the parser,
not the lexer, and the C lexer shows only the surviving transitions. Collect the
dropped transitions in `build_lex_table`, along with each lexer state's valid
tokens and parse states, and add `--report-lex-states`:

- `-` prints one line per dropped transition with how many lexer states and
  parse states it affects, extras first (the surprising class).
- `*` prints every lexer state a parse state starts in: its parse states, valid
  tokens and dropped transitions.
- `N` prints lexer state `N`.

The report is opt-in and does not affect the generated tables.
